## Why This Question Is Asked
This question assesses practical memory management skills and understanding of lock-free data structures—essential competencies for systems programming and performance-critical applications.

## Key Concepts
- **Lock-free free-list design**: Using an atomic head pointer enables concurrent allocation/deallocation without locks
- **Memory layout**: Fixed-size 64-byte blocks with the next pointer as the first field ensure proper alignment on 64-bit systems
- **ABA problem**: Tagged pointers (using the unused bits in a 64-bit pointer) can prevent ABA issues in high-contention scenarios
- **Contention testing**: Multi-threaded stress tests verify correctness under concurrent access patterns

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block {
    struct Block* next;
    unsigned char payload[56];
} Block;

static _Atomic(Block*) free_head;

Block* allocate(void) {
    Block* head;
    Block* new_head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        if (!head) return NULL;
        new_head = head->next;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, new_head,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
    return head;
}

void free_block(void* ptr) {
    Block* block = (Block*)ptr;
    Block* head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        block->next = head;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, block,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
}
```

## Test Plan (4 Threads)
1. **Initialization**: Preallocate all N blocks, verify free_head points to the full list
2. **Basic correctness**: Single-threaded allocate/free sequences, verify all blocks returned
3. **Concurrent stress**: 4 threads repeatedly allocate and free blocks; track allocation counts to ensure no leaks or double-frees
4. **Boundary conditions**: Allocate until empty, verify NULL returned; free all blocks, verify full recovery
5. **Data integrity**: Write patterns to payload, free, allocate fresh blocks, verify previous data is not present

